hi there steve kaufman um i'm going to  do two videos today  uh in answer to two of the most common  questions or comments that i get  both here on my youtube channel and at  my blog the linguist on language blog or  even that link  the first question which i can deal with  pretty quickly is  does it make sense to study more than  one language at the same time  the second question is  uh how much of an advantage was it that  i was born in sweden spoke swedish till  i was five  and then moved to canada  how much of an advantage was that to my  language learning so i'll deal with the  second question in a separate video and  i'll address the question of studying  two languages at the same time  my answer to this is usually  that i have a lot of trouble studying  two languages at the same time  i find that you need a lot of  intensity  to be successful at language learning  you have to there's a sort of a  transformation that takes place you have  to create some habits some language  habits in the brain  and so for that reason alone i like to  concentrate on one language  um  i find that i get hooked on the language  that i'm studying  i've said in one of my one of my pieces  that learning a language is like falling  in love it's difficult to fall in love  with two people at the same time for  most people so i tend to get hooked on  the one language  as an example right now i'm still with  my russian and i'm trying to  steal time away from it for korean i'm  finding it difficult to do  uh it takes some special motivation like  prior to my trip to  portugal i was able to take time away  from russian and i spent two or three  months on  portuguese um  the uh you know recently i was gonna do  some videos in in spanish so i i got a  hold of some spanish  uh audio books on link and read them and  listen to them  uh so i i often say you know at the very  least i want to spend 80 percent of my  time on my major language and perhaps 20  on the minor and typically the minor  language has to be one that i'm already  fairly well along in  i can't start um a language from scratch  and hope to get anywhere if i'm only  spending 20 percent of my time on it  that's me other people may  you know like doing things differently  one other thing i should say about  studying more than one language is  if i wherever i do take time away from a  language which i've done in the past  like um  you know when i was on i can't remember  what the cantonese i took time away to  really focus on swedish i did a lot of  listening to swedish audiobooks and  reading because i was going to sweden  when i come back from that period of  intensive exposure to swedish  all of my languages improved  time that i i take a break from language  a and focus on language b for a month  three months six months  a year  uh all the languages improve so i i very  definitely believe  that a that any language that you work  at intensively  uh particularly with a a big focus on on  on input-based learning and also of  using it  uh rather than sort of trying to learn  rules and isolated word lists so if  you're really in the language  uh  whatever progress you achieve you're  going to keep that's been my experience  a period of what i called in one post  at my blog benign neglect is not going  to damage you you'll lose a bit of  traction you'll forget a few words but  when you get really back into it you'll  do better than before so in summary i  find it difficult to study two languages  at the same time  i tend to focus at least eighty percent  on one  the other twenty percent i can only  really use to refresh languages  that where i already you know i am  fairly well along  i could not undertake a new language or  one where i'm at a very low level like  my korean uh just by spending 20 of my  time on it i have i need more  uh but so my recommendation is to go in  in spurts  uh six months on one  then you can go six months on another  uh it's been my experience and if you do  that you won't lose ground really in the  first one in fact you'll probably  improve in it so there you have it  that's my answer on whether it makes  sense to study more than one language at  the same time thank you for listening
 

